keenable.snippet:
Previous systematic reviews and meta-analyses published on creatine and soccer performance have generally addressed narrower eligibility questions and included smaller evidence sets. For instance, the review conducted by Mielgo-Ayuso et al. (2019) included nine trials [18], while more recently Guevara et al. (2024) incorporated six studies [19], reflecting a narrower scope rather than necessarily a methodological deficiency.

Studies were included if they met the following criteria:
randomised or non-randomised controlled clinical trials;
conducted on male or female soccer players of any age or competitive level;
investigated creatine supplementation as the primary intervention, regardless of dosage or duration;
included a placebo, control, or comparison condition; and
reported at least one outcome related to performance (e.g. sprint, power, jump performance), recovery/fatigue (e.g. markers of muscle damage, fatigue index, lactate), or body composition (e.g. fat mass, lean mass, body weight).

